.TH SYSTEMD-COMPOSE 1 "" "systemd-compose" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
systemd-compose \- run a project's services as systemd user units, with docker compose's verbs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B systemd-compose
.RB [ \-\-system | \-s | \-\-user ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-profile
.IR NAME ]...\&
.I VERB
.RI [ ARGS ...]
.br
.B systemd-compose help
.RI [ VERB ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B systemd-compose
runs a project's services as systemd user units, with the verbs of docker
compose and a project-local
.BR systemd-compose.yaml .
.PP
.B Inside a project,
every verb acts on that project, on the user instance.
The project is the
.B systemd-compose.yaml
in the current directory or the nearest parent, or the file that
.B \-f
names.
.B up
renders unit files from the yaml, registers them with the user manager and
starts them.
.B down
stops and unregisters them.
The other verbs act on the project's units alone.
.PP
.B Outside a project,
the verbs act on the user instance through
.BR systemctl (1)
and
.BR journalctl (1).
.B \-\-system
acts on the system instance instead, which is the default for root.
.PP
It is ordinary systemd underneath:
.B systemctl \-\-user
and
.B journalctl \-\-user
can inspect or undo anything it does.
A verb this program does not know passes through to
.BR systemctl .
.SS The project name
Every unit a project renders carries its name:
.IB PROJECT \- SERVICE .service
for each service, with its
.B .socket
or
.BR .timer ;
.IB PROJECT .target ,
which starts them together; and
.IB PROJECT .slice ,
which they run in.
Two copies of one project can run on one machine under two names.
The name comes from, in order:
.IP 1. 4
.B \-p
.I NAME
.IP 2. 4
.B SYSTEMD_COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME
in the environment
.IP 3. 4
the same variable in the
.B .env
beside the yaml
.IP 4. 4
.B name:
in the yaml
.IP 5. 4
the directory's name
.PP
A name may not contain a dash.
A directory named
.B my-app
gives the name
.BR my_app .
.SS Profiles
A service with
.B profiles:
runs only while one of its profiles is active.
A profile is activated with
.B \-\-profile
.I NAME
before the verb, which may be repeated, or else with
.B SYSTEMD_COMPOSE_PROFILES
(comma-separated) in the environment or the
.BR .env .
.B \-\-profile '*'
activates all of them.
.B down
and a bare
.B stop
act on every profile.
A service named on the command line is acted on whatever its profile.
.SH OPTIONS
These go before the verb; a verb's own flags go after it.
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE" "\fR, \fP\-\-file " FILE
The project's yaml, instead of looking for
.B systemd-compose.yaml
in the current directory and its parents.
One file: a second
.B \-f
is refused.
.TP
.BI \-p " NAME" "\fR, \fP\-\-project\-name " NAME
The project name, before every other source.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME"
Activate a profile; repeatable;
.B \(aq*\(aq
is all of them.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-system
The system instance.
Refused inside a project: projects live on the user instance.
.TP
.B \-\-user
The user instance, for root outside a project.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
The help text.
.TP
.B \-\-version
The same as
.BR version .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Inside a project
.TP
.B up \fR[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-build\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-\-force\-recreate\fR|\fB\-\-no\-recreate\fR] [\fB\-\-wait\-timeout\fR \fIT\fR]
Check every rendered unit with
.BR "systemd-analyze verify" ,
then print the plan.
Run the
.B build:
steps whose
.B creates:
path is missing.
Register the units, start the project, and restart the services that
changed.
.B up
exits 1, naming the unit, when one failed to start, keeps restarting, or did
not start because a service it requires is not up.
A service counts as started once its program has run 3 seconds.
One that restarted on the way up is watched for up to 15 seconds.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
The plan and any refusal; nothing is built, written, registered or started.
.TP
.B \-\-build
Run every service's build, with
.B creates:
or without.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Retire running units the yaml no longer declares (refused without it).
.TP
.B \-\-force\-recreate
Restart every running service, changed or not.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-recreate
Restart none; a changed one stays
.B changed (not applied)
for the next
.BR up .
.TP
.BI \-\-wait\-timeout " T"
Watch a service that restarts on the way up for
.I T
instead of 15s (a bare number is seconds; 90s, 2min); one with a
healthcheck is watched for as long as its healthcheck may take, when that
is longer.
.PP
Neither recreate flag touches an
.B on_change: start-only
service, which
.B up
never restarts.
.BR \-d ,
.B \-\-detach
and
.B \-\-wait
are accepted:
.B up
is always detached, and waits for every service.
.RE
.TP
.B down
Stop and unregister every unit of the project, and stop any
.B run
or
.B build
still going.
Units that an older version of the yaml registered from this directory are
retired too, running or not.
The rendered files stay in
.BR .systemd-compose/ .
.TP
.B ps
The project's units and their state, with a
.B HEALTH
column for each service with a healthcheck, the next run of each scheduled
job, and any one-off still running.
.B \-a
is accepted.
.TP
.B logs \fR[\fB\-f\fR] [\fB\-\-tail\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-since\fR \fIT\fR] [\fB\-t\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-log\-prefix\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-color\fR] [\fISERVICE\fR...]
The project's journal, through
.BR "journalctl \-\-user" .
For a
.IR SERVICE ,
it shows the service's unit with its socket and timer.
With none, it shows every unit of the project and its slice, with systemd's
own lines about each.
.B \-\-tail
.I N
is
.B \-n
.IR N ,
and
.B \-\-tail all
has no limit.
.B \-\-since
and
.B \-\-until
take compose's
.B 10m
as well as journalctl's times.
.B \-\-no\-log\-prefix
is
.BR "\-o cat" .
.B \-t
is accepted; every line carries a timestamp anyway.
Any other flag is journalctl's
.RB ( \-f ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-p ...).
It also shows a line printed by a child process that exits at once, which
.B journalctl \-u
misses, and it needs no running user manager.
.TP
.BR start | stop | restart " \fR[\fISERVICE\fR...]"
Change the run state only; the units stay registered.
With no
.IR SERVICE ,
they act on every service.
Each says what started or stopped, including dependents.
.B start
and
.B restart
clear a named service's failed state first.
They exit 1 when a service does not come up, or when a name given has
nothing registered to start.
.B stop
exits 1 when a name given is running but is not this project's, and leaves
it running.
.TP
.B kill \fR[\fB\-s\fR \fISIGNAL\fR] \fISERVICE\fR...
Send a signal to the service's processes: SIGTERM, or the one that
.B \-s
.RB ( \-\-signal )
names, by name or number.
Compose's kill sends SIGKILL; use
.B "\-s KILL"
for that.
Like
.BR stop ,
it exits 1 for a name that runs but is not this project's.
.TP
.B build \fR[\fISERVICE\fR...]
Run every step of the named services'
.BR build: .
With no
.IR SERVICE ,
run the build of every active service that has one.
Steps run as transient units in the project's slice, in the service's
working directory, environment and env files.
After
.B build
.IR SERVICE ,
.B restart
.I SERVICE
runs the new build.
.TP
.B run \fR[\fB\-e\fR \fIKEY\fB=\fIVAL\fR] [\fB\-w\fR \fIDIR\fR] [\fB\-T\fR] \fISERVICE\fR [\fICMD\fR...]
.PD 0
.TP
.B exec \fR[\fB\-e\fR \fIKEY\fB=\fIVAL\fR] [\fB\-w\fR \fIDIR\fR] [\fB\-T\fR] \fISERVICE CMD\fR...
.PD
Run a one-off command as a transient unit, in the service's working
directory, environment and env files.
With no
.IR CMD ,
.B run
runs the service's own command, so
.B run
.I JOB
runs a scheduled job now.
.B exec
is
.B run
with the command required.
.B \-e
.RB ( \-\-env )
adds a variable, which wins over the service's env files.
.B \-w
.RB ( \-\-workdir )
sets the directory, relative to the service's.
.B \-T
.RB ( \-\-no\-TTY )
runs without a pty; otherwise a terminal gets one.
Compose's
.BR \-\-rm ,
.B \-\-no\-deps
and
.B \-i
are accepted.
The one-off runs under the service's own
.BR resources: .
While the project is registered it runs in the project's slice, and
otherwise it takes the project's
.B resources:
too.
No other service is started.
A one-off whose client goes away is stopped with it.
.TP
.B config
The yaml, then every unit as it would be rendered.
It needs no user manager.
.TP
.B top \fR[\fISERVICE\fR] [\fIARGS\fR...]
.BR systemd-cgtop (1)
on the project's slice, or on one service's processes.
.I ARGS
are cgtop's own
.RB ( "\-n 1" ).
.TP
.B list-timers
.B systemctl list\-timers
on the project's scheduled jobs: when each runs next, and last ran.
.TP
.I VERB \fR[\fISERVICE\fR...]
Any other
.B systemctl
verb, run as
.B systemctl \-\-user
.I VERB
with each service name mapped to its unit.
A scheduled job's unit is its timer.
.BR status ,
.BR cat ,
.BR reset\-failed ,
.B list\-dependencies
and
.B clean
also take the job's own service, and
.B is\-failed
takes only that.
A verb that would act on the whole instance without a unit
.RB ( status ,
.BR show ,
.BR cat ,
.BR is\-active ...)
needs a service named.
A verb that changes units
.RB ( reset\-failed ,
.BR freeze ,
.BR clean ,
.BR set\-property ,
.BR reload ...)
takes only this project's services.
.BR enable ,
.BR disable ,
.BR reenable ,
.B mask
and
.B preset
are refused, because
.B up
and
.B down
own the registration.
.B preset\-all
and a
.B cancel
with no job named are refused too, because they act on the whole instance.
.SS Anywhere
.TP
.B ls
Every project registered on the user instance: its name, how many of its
services run (and restart or failed), and its yaml; a project whose
directory or yaml is gone says so.
It works inside a project too.
.TP
.BR version ", " \-\-version
This program's version, then systemd's.
.TP
.B help \fR[\fIVERB\fR]
The help text, or one verb's lines.
.I VERB
.B \-\-help
shows them too, for one of this program's verbs.
.SS Outside a project
On the user instance (the system instance when run as root, or with
.BR \-\-system ).
.TP
.B ps \fR[\fB\-a\fR] [\fIPATTERN\fR]
.BR "systemctl list\-units \-\-type=service,timer" ;
.BR \-a :
stopped units too.
.TP
.B logs \fR[\fB\-f\fR] [\fIUNIT\fR...]
.BR journalctl ,
one
.B \-u
per
.IR UNIT ;
with none, the whole journal.
It needs no running manager.
.TP
.BR start | stop | restart " \fIUNIT\fR..."
The same words in
.BR systemctl .
.TP
.B up \fIUNIT\fR...
.BR "systemctl enable \-\-now" :
register for boot and start.
.TP
.B down \fIUNIT\fR...
.BR "systemctl disable \-\-now" :
stop and unregister; the unit file stays.
.TP
.B config \fIUNIT\fR...
.BR "systemctl cat" .
.TP
.B top
.B systemd-cgtop
on the instance's cgroup subtree.
.TP
.I VERB ARGS\fR...
.B systemctl
.I VERB ARGS
as given.
.PP
.BR build ,
.B run
and
.B exec
need a project.
.SH THE PROJECT FILE
.B systemd-compose.yaml
has three top-level keys:
.B name
(the project name),
.B resources
(caps on the whole project, as for a service) and
.BR services .
A key starting with
.B x\-
is ignored; it holds yaml anchors for merge keys.
A service takes:
.TP 16
.B command
the program and its arguments
.RB ( ExecStart= ),
a string or a list; the program is resolved against
.B ~/.local/bin
and
.B PATH
when the units are rendered
.TP
.B entrypoint
words put in front of
.B command
.TP
.B working_dir
.BR WorkingDirectory= ,
relative to the yaml; default the project directory
.TP
.B environment
.B Environment=
lines, a map or a list of
.IB KEY = value
.TP
.B env_file
.B EnvironmentFile=
files, each a path or
.RB { path ,
.BR required }
.TP
.B restart
.BR no ,
.BR on\-failure ,
.B always
or
.BR unless\-stopped ,
or
.RB { policy ,
.BR delay }
.TP
.B depends_on
a list of services, or a map of
.RB { condition ,
.BR required ,
.BR restart }.
.B condition
is
.B service_started
(the default),
.B service_healthy
or
.B service_completed_successfully
.TP
.B healthcheck
.RB { test ,
.BR interval ,
.BR timeout ,
.BR start_period }:
a probe that gates the service's start
.TP
.B oneshot
a job that ends, which dependents can wait for
.TP
.B schedule
a timer that runs the service: an
.B OnCalendar=
expression, or
.RB { calendar ,
.BR accuracy ,
.BR persistent ,
.BR randomized_delay }
.TP
.B build
.RB { run ,
.BR creates }:
the steps that make the program
.TP
.B resources
.RB { memory ,
.BR cpus ,
.BR pids }
for the service.
.B cpus
needs the cpu controller delegated to your user manager
.TP
.B on_change
.B restart
(the default) or
.BR start\-only :
never restarted by
.B up
.TP
.B listen
socket activation: the addresses of a
.B .socket
unit
.TP
.B profiles
the profiles the service belongs to
.TP
.B unit
raw systemd sections
.RB ( Unit ,
.BR Service ,
.BR Timer ,
.BR Socket ),
merged last.
.BR Environment= ,
.B EnvironmentFile=
and
.B WorkingDirectory=
are refused: use the keys, which build and run get too
.PP
Values may use compose's interpolation
.RB ( $VAR ,
.BR ${VAR:\-default} ...),
except keys and anything under
.BR unit: ,
which is systemd's own syntax.
The variables come from the
.B .env
beside the yaml, never from the shell.
.B $$
is a literal
.BR $ .
.PP
In
.BR command ,
.BR entrypoint ,
.BR environment ,
.B healthcheck
and
.BR listen ,
a
.B %
starts a systemd specifier
.RB ( %h ,
.BR %t ...),
expanded when the unit runs.
.B %%
is a literal
.BR % .
.BR working_dir ,
.BR env_file ,
.B build: creates:
and
.B schedule:
take no
.BR % .
In a service with
.BR build: ,
.B environment
takes no specifier either, because build steps run through
.BR systemd\-run ,
which expands none.
.PP
.B docs/config.example.yaml
uses every key, with its rules.
Also at
.br
https://github.com/xflash96/systemd\-compose/blob/main/docs/config.example.yaml
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SYSTEMD_COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME
The project name.
The section
.B The project name
above gives its place among the other sources.
.TP
.B SYSTEMD_COMPOSE_PROFILES
The active profiles, comma-separated, when no
.B \-\-profile
is given; also read from the
.BR .env .
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
How
.B systemctl \-\-user
reaches the user manager.
Outside a login session (cron,
.BR "su user \-c" ),
set it to
.BI /run/user/ UID\fR.
.TP
.B PATH
Where a service's program is found when the units are rendered, after
.BR ~/.local/bin .
Nothing else from the shell's environment reaches a service.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B systemd-compose.yaml
The project, in the current directory or a parent.
.TP
.B .env
Beside the yaml: the variables for interpolation, and
.B SYSTEMD_COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME
and
.BR SYSTEMD_COMPOSE_PROFILES .
.TP
.B .systemd-compose/
Beside the yaml: the rendered units, which
.B up
writes and links; it ignores itself in git.
.TP
.B ~/.config/systemd/user/
The links
.B up
makes to the rendered units, and
.BI default.target.wants/ PROJECT .target\fR,
which starts the project at boot (with lingering).
.TP
.BI ~/.config/systemd/user/ PROJECT \-.service.d/
Drop-ins for every service of the project;
.IB PROJECT \- SERVICE .service.d/
for one, and
.IB PROJECT \-.timer.d/
for its timers.
.BR up 's
verify step reads them, but a change to one restarts nothing.
.TP
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/systemd-compose/
Copies of this program, one per build, that the units' healthcheck probes
run, so moving or replacing the program breaks no probe
.RB ( ~/.local/share/systemd-compose/
by default).
.TP
.B $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/systemd-compose/
The lock that keeps one
.BR up ,
.BR down ,
.BR build ,
.B start
or
.B restart
of a project at a time, and the files a run keeps while it works: its
verify copies, a one-off's environment and how it ended, each named with
its process's pid and swept once that process is gone.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A verb failed; or
.BR up ,
.B start
or
.B restart
found a unit that did not come up, or
.B stop
or
.B kill
was given a name that runs but is not this project's.
.PP
A verb passed through to
.BR systemctl ,
.B journalctl
or
.B systemd-cgtop
exits with that program's status, as that program replaces this one.
A project's own
.BR stop ,
.BR start ,
.B restart
or
.B kill
passes on
.BR systemctl 's
status, too, when systemctl itself failed and the report said nothing about
the unit (5 for a unit systemd no longer has loaded, say).
.B run
and
.B exec
exit with the command's status, as a shell reports it.
.B 130
is a ^C at the command's terminal, a signal to this client, or a closed
terminal; the command is stopped.
128 plus the signal's number is a command killed by a signal, which standard
error names.
.B 137
is a command killed at its memory cap, and standard error says it ran out of
memory.
.B 255
is a command stopped before it finished
.RB ( down ,
.BR "systemctl stop" ),
or one that exited 255 itself.
.SH NOTES
A project runs on your systemd user instance, which by default stops at your
last logout and does not start at boot.
For a project that should keep running, enable lingering once per machine
.RB ( up
warns while it is off):
.PP
.RS
.EX
loginctl enable\-linger
.EE
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
In the directory of a project named
.B demo
with a service
.BR api :
.PP
.EX
systemd\-compose up \-\-dry\-run   # the plan; nothing written or started
systemd\-compose up               # render, verify, build, register, start
systemd\-compose ps
systemd\-compose logs \-f api
systemd\-compose run api npm run migrate
systemd\-compose status api       # systemctl \-\-user status demo\-api.service
systemd\-compose \-p demo2 up      # a second copy, named demo2
systemd\-compose \-p demo2 down    # each copy comes down under its own name
systemd\-compose down
.EE
